      "problem": "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-16 & =0 \\\\ 16-2\\lambda -8\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =10,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{10} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
