    {
      "dom_id": "question-95286",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Any $6\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $6$ distinct singular values.\n\nII. Any $8\\times 9$ matrix $A$ has at most $9$ distinct singular values.\n\nIII. The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ has at most two non-negative distinct singular values.",
      "worked_solution": "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $6\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $8\\times 9$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $9\\times 8$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $9\\times 9$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{9\\times 8}\\times {A}_{8\\times 9}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{9\\times 9}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $9$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement III is true.  Indeed, all singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
