      "name": null,
      "problem": "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2\\end{aligned}]?$",
      "worked_solution": "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda -4\\lambda +36-36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -13) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =13,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{13} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
